{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:4fd97eb5f44fa32150e7fea06cb563aa93b2c651f66747e72c0b802da6a1d2aa","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.1},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.05},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.2}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: the Historian's 0.4 / 1.75 / 3.1, anchored on the claim that the current level coincides with the 1960-2025 unconditional mean and that the Reinhart-Sbrancia modal resolution of >100%-of-GDP debt episodes is financial repression at low or negative real rates. THIS IS THE CQ WHERE MY UPDATING MOVES MOST, AND IT IS THE PRIOR'S LEFT TAIL THAT BREAKS.\nSTARTING LEVEL. Locked: US 10y TIPS ~2.60%, FRED DFII10, 30 Oct 2026. G7 GDP weights (market FX) US ~0.50, Japan ~0.11, Germany ~0.11, UK ~0.08, France ~0.08, Italy ~0.06, Canada ~0.05; against reals of roughly 2.60 / 0.30 / 0.60 / 1.30-1.50 / 1.00-1.30 / 1.60-2.20 / 1.60, the weighted start is 1.78-1.82%. I use 1.80%. The US supplies about 1.30pp of that, so this is roughly 72% a US TIPS question. The non-US legs are NOT independently verified at my horizon: the Scout located a 10y JGB \"rose to a 30-year high\" Reuters item of 1 Sep 2026 without a level, a 2.06% JGB print described as a 26-year high in Dec-2025, and an UNDATED 2.635% bund level it correctly refused to rely on. I carry that limitation.\nUPDATE 1, THE DOMINANT ONE. The locked 2.60% TIPS print is itself the highest-LR evidence in the entire question set, and the Historian treated it as a coincidence rather than as evidence. P(US 10y TIPS = 2.60% on 30 Oct 2026 | the G7 GDP-weighted 2032-36 average is >= 1.5%) / P(same | the average is < 1.5%) = about 3.0. Justification: real yields are highly persistent at a five-to-six-year horizon (decade-average shifts of 100-300bp happen, but they happen slowly and from the prevailing level, not around it), and a 2.60% US real yield is the highest sustained level since 2008. ABOVE 2:1, KEPT AT FULL WEIGHT. Arithmetic consequence: reaching the Historian's P10 of 0.40% as a FIVE-YEAR AVERAGE requires the G7-weighted real yield to fall roughly 140bp from the start AND stay there, which requires the US collapsing to ~0.5% real AND no Japanese or German normalization at all. Prior odds on \"P10 region\" of about 0.11 divided by 3.0 gives 0.037 — the tail does not survive. P10 moves from 0.40 to 1.10.\nUPDATE 2. The Scout's JGB signal: 10y JGB at a 30-year high as of 1 Sep 2026, with a 2.06% nominal print described as a 26-year high in Dec-2025. LR for \"the Japanese leg normalizes up over 2032-36\" = about 2.2. ABOVE 2:1, KEPT. Japan is 0.11 of the weight at roughly 0.30% real; normalization to 1.0-1.3% real adds +0.08 to +0.11pp to the weighted average on its own, and it does so in a way that is largely independent of the US cycle. Germany at 0.11 and ~0.60% real adds a similar amount. This is why my P50 can rise without the US going above 2.60%.\nUPDATE 3, DISCOUNTED. Hyperscaler capex still accelerating: 2026 forecasts of $803-828B with Amazon signalling \"meaningfully more in 2027\", Alphabet 2026 raised to $180-190B and 2027 to increase \"significantly\", Moody's at ~$785B closing on $1T in 2027. Caveat preserved: the $803-828B and the earlier ~$725B Big-4 figures use different company sets and dates and are not directly comparable. LR for a sustained real capital-demand bid of about 1.6 — BELOW 2:1, and critically it is undercut by the locked AI-Q4 at 66%, which WITHDRAWS that capital demand inside the very window being forecast. DISCOUNTED to 1.15; worth +0.05pp.\nSTRUCTURAL LADDER, ADOPTED FROM THE FERMI SEAT AS INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION RATHER THAN AS EVIDENCE. Delta r* nets to approximately ZERO: life-cycle dissaving +0.35 (old-age ratio 31% -> 39-41%) and public dissaving +0.25 and AI/grid capital demand +0.15 (already probability-weighted at 0.34 x 0.45, small precisely because of AI-Q4) against precautionary saving -0.35, cognitive-labour compression -0.10 (AI-Q5 at 0.382 times a -0.30pp effect) and lower g -0.35 (my 1.75% coupled growth against a historical ~2.6%, at a 0.5-0.7 pass-through). The non-obvious result stands: this is NOT an r* story. The real term premium widens +0.40pp (eroding sovereign convenience yields; the marginal duration holder is a price-SENSITIVE leveraged basis-trade account rather than a price-insensitive reserve manager; 3-year P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% of GDP, +2.4pp more under a 20% AI-led equity shock; a positive equity-bond correlation means duration that stops hedging must pay for itself). A repression wedge of -0.20pp (0.35 probability of a material repression regime times a -0.60pp wedge) preserves the Reinhart-Sbrancia content of the prior at its correct, reduced weight. 1.80 - 0.05 + 0.40 - 0.20 = 1.95; plus the Japan/Germany normalization increment that the ladder books only partially gives 2.05%.\nP90 = 3.2: fiscal dominance with a term-premium spike, no repression, Japan fully normalized, G7 reals above the current US TIPS level; scaled below a 1980s-size shift because the inflation base is far lower. A five-year average compresses tails, which is why I do not exceed 3.2.\nTHE SIMULATION: not identified for this question and I take ZERO point estimate from it. capital.interestRate is an economy-wide real return on capital (an MPK), there is no 10y sovereign benchmark, no termPremium anywhere in src/, and no breakeven or inflation swap. The only content I use is the cross-scenario ORDERING of its debtRiskPremium (tech-breakthrough lowest at 1.20pp, debt-populism highest at 2.50pp with the real rate at 6.96%), LR about 1.3, DISCOUNTED, supporting an upper-half rather than a lower-half answer and nothing else. Low-fertility-conditional.\nDIRECTION: AMPLIFIED upward. P50 +0.30pp and P10 +0.70pp off the Historian prior — the largest revision in my payload, and it comes from a single high-LR observation (the locked TIPS anchor) applied against a base rate (financial repression) that the prior had weighted as modal rather than as a 0.35-probability branch.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.427Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% year-on-year for four consecutive quarters. This directly tests the composition argument (Turkey, Mexico, Colombia, Chile, Costa Rica holding the aggregate above the G7 core) that is the load-bearing non-AI assumption in my whole payload.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 falls from 40 to about 24 (conjunct A from 0.72 to roughly 0.45). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 26 down to about 15, D from 14 up to about 24, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix the regime probabilities shift mass from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional holding to one of my best. This is the single cheapest observation that falsifies me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 (US 10y TIPS) 12-month moving average falls below 1.20% at any point, i.e. roughly 140bp below the locked 30 Oct 2026 print of 2.60%, without an accompanying MSCI World drawdown of >= 25% (so it is a level shift, not a flight-to-quality spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ4 P50 falls from 2.05 to about 1.50 and P10 from 1.10 to about 0.55, restoring the Historian's Reinhart-Sbrancia repression tail that my 3:1 likelihood ratio on the TIPS anchor currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 22 to about 15 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. In the asset matrix G7 long nominals and TIPS improve across all four regimes and my 2026-2030 buildout numbers for cash and ILBs are too high.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks, and gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting (against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026).\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The 2.5:1 likelihood ratio that does most of the downward work in my CQ3 collapses. CQ3 P50 rises from 1.75 to about 1.95 and P90 from 2.65 back to about 2.90; CQ6-A rises from 11 to about 16-17 and B falls correspondingly. It also raises realized AI-Q3 datacentre load back toward the locked 998 TWh P50 and cuts my upward correction to AI-Q4. Power/grid and construction conditional returns fall (the scarcity rent disappears), AI-complex conditional returns rise.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS monthly release) 12-month average exceeds 2.0% for four consecutive quarters, with core ex-food-and-energy above 1.8% — i.e. the PPI +5.0% signal of Sep-2026 transmits to the household price level rather than decoupling as it did in 2021.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 conjunct B (0.62) is wrong and CQ2 falls from 40 to about 22. It also means the Chinese demand deficit I have priced is resolving, which raises my China growth leg above 2.8%, lifts CQ3 P50 by roughly 0.10-0.15pp through the 0.335 PPP weight, and improves China equities across every regime while worsening onshore duration.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey or the OECD earnings database shows ISIC F+Q real median hourly wage growth leading J+K+M by >= 3.0 percentage points cumulative since 2025 (i.e. on pace for the +10pp gate), rather than the dead heat the Atlanta Fed tracker currently shows at 4.2 against 4.2.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ1 rises from 9 to about 25 (conjunct C2 from 0.15 to roughly 0.45), and the Stanford/ADP hiring-volume-not-pay mechanism that is my only above-2:1 signal on that question is falsified. It also means physical scarcity is clearing by PRICE rather than by rationing, which raises CQ6-C from 26 toward 32 through the cost-push channel and makes construction/trades and healthcare operators materially better holdings than I have priced in regimes B and C.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'growth'."}}